## A. Persona Perspective Summary

I am an ICB commissioner in England, accountable for the health of a local population of ~1.5 million. My board is facing a predictable but increasingly severe winter crisis: A&E 4‑hour performance below 65%, ambulance handover delays exceeding 90 minutes, rising respiratory admissions (RSV, flu, COVID‑19), and a backlog of elective care that worsens when beds are blocked. I must allocate limited marginal funding (£10–30 million in non‑recurrent winter resilience funds) across interventions that simultaneously reduce immediate pressure, shift care upstream, and begin the three mandated NHS shifts (prevention, community, digital). My primary constraints are time (decisions needed before October), workforce availability, and public expectation. I am also measured on reducing inequalities: patients in the most deprived quintile have 3× the emergency admission rate for ambulatory‑care‑sensitive conditions (e.g. COPD, diabetes). The analysis below reflects my real‑world trade‑offs: high composite scores go to large‑effect, evidence‑backed interventions that can be scaled rapidly, even if they are not the most innovative. I acknowledge that a patient‑facing charity or a clinical specialist would likely rank priorities differently.

