## Part 2: Priority‑by‑Priority Evidence‑Based Intervention List

### Priority 1: Expanded Influenza + COVID‑19 + RSV Vaccination

**Intervention 1.1: Opt‑out vaccination for hospitalised older adults (respiratory admissions)**
- **Implementation level**: Clinical (within hospital discharge process)
- **Mechanism**: Administer vaccine during index admission or before discharge to prevent next exacerbation.
- **Resources**: Low (vaccine cost ~£20/dose; nurse time 10 minutes) + coverage of all eligible patients on a given ward
- **Implementation requirements**: protocol for offering vaccine to all >65 admitted; real‑time electronic flag
- **Strength of evidence**: Strong (RCTs showing reduced readmission, e.g. in flu)
- **Feasibility timeline**: Within 1 year
- **Barries**: patient refusal; logistics of ordering vaccines for inpatients; competing discharge priorities
- **Metrics**: % eligible vaccinated during admission; readmission rate for pneumonia/flu

**Intervention 1.2: Community‑based pop‑up clinics in deprived areas with mobile GP teams**
- **Implementation level**: Policy & organisational (ICB commissioning of outreach teams)
- **Mechanism**: Locate clinics at community centres, mosques, libraries to increase access in areas with <50% uptake
- **Resources**: Medium (travel, staff, comms) – approx. £15/patient
- **Implementation**: Map uptake by LSOA; target 10 worst postcodes; use link workers
- **Evidence**: Observational (UKHSA 2023) shows outreach increase uptake by 5–15%
- **Timeline**: within 6 months setup
- **Barries**: vaccine hesitancy in minoritised groups; require trusted messengers
- **Metrics**: difference in uptake between most/least deprived quintile

**Intervention 1.3: Mandatory seasonal vaccination for all frontline NHS staff**
- **Implementation level**: Policy (national mandate)
- **Mechanism**: Reduces nosocomial transmission to vulnerable inpatients
- **Resources**: Low (vaccine procurement; mandate enforcement)
- **Evidence**: Observational – reduces staff sick leave and patient mortality (Cochrane 2018 review)
- **Timeline**: 2 years to enforce
- **Barriers**: union opposition; opt‑out rates still high
- **Metrics**: staff uptake rate; HA‑influenza cases

### Priority 2: Hospital‑at‑Home / Virtual Wards for Acute Respiratory Conditions

**Intervention 2.1: Acute respiratory virtual ward (COPD exacerbation)**
- **Implementation level**: Organisational (trust‑based expansion of existing virtual ward teams)
- **Mechanism**: Daily remote monitoring of O₂ saturation, symptoms; daily clinical call; enable antibiotics/steroids at home
- **Resources**: Medium (software platform, pulse oximeters, virtual ward coordinators) – setup cost 200‑500k for 50 beds
- **Evidence**: RCT in UK (Reading) showed 30% reduction in ED to admitted conversion; non‑inferiority for mortality
- **Timeline**: scalable within 3–6 months if infrastructure exists
- **Barriers**: capacity of community nursing; digital exclusion (provide equipment)
- **Metrics**: bed‑days saved, 30‑day readmission rate, patient satisfaction

**Intervention 2.2: Infection‑based virtual ward for COVID‑19 high‑risk patients**
- **Implementation level**: Clinical/Organisational (ICB commissioned)
- **Mechanism**: Remote pulse ox + antiviral delivery; escalation pathway to hospital
- **Resources**: Low (existing Oximetry@Home model from pandemic)
- **Evidence**: Observational – reduced hospitalisation by 25% (NHS England evaluation)
- **Timeline**: reactivate immediately
- **Barriers**: vaccine hesitancy but not a barrier here
- **Metrics**: % escalations prevented, mortality

### Priority 3: Proactive Frailty Identification + CGA in Primary Care/ED

**Intervention 3.1: Risk‑stratified GP case‑finding using the Electronic Frailty Index (eFI)**
- **Implementation level**: Clinical / Organisational (GP practices)
- **Mechanism**: Flag moderate‑severe frailty patients; offer CGA in surgery or via community geriatrician
- **Resources**: Low (software, practice nurse time 30 min per patient)
- **Evidence**: eFI has good predictive validity; cluster RCT found CGA reduced admissions by 20% in this group (Eklund et al., 2017)
- **Timeline**: within 3 month to set up risk score; effects within 12 months
- **Barriers**: time pressure in primary care; willingness to use eFI
- **Metrics**: number of CGA performed; ED attendance per 1000 frail patients

**Intervention 3.2: ED‑based geriatrician co‑assessment for all patients >75 presenting with falls or confusion**
- **Implementation level**: Clinical (hospital ED)
- **Mechanism**: Immediate CGA by specialist team; redirect ~30% from admission to frailty SDEC
- **Resources**: Medium (need 2 WTE geriatrician + OT per major ED) - cost ~£250k – saved ~£500k in bed‑days
- **Evidence**: Meta‑analysis (Ellis et al. 2011, update) – reduced mortality and admission
- **Timeline**: within 1 year when frailty team is trained
- **Barriers**: senior workforce scarcity; ED space
- **Metrics**: admission rate for frail patients; 30‑day readmission

### Priority 4: Same‑Day Emergency Care (SDEC)

**Intervention 4.1: Respiratory SDEC unit (e.g., asthma, COPD exacerbation, pneumonia)**
- **Implementation level**: Organisational (trust‑based)
- **Mechanism**: dedicated ambulatory unit where patients receive iv antibiotics, neubuliser, observation for up to 6–12 h, then discharge
- **Resources**: Medium (3–6 trolley beds, Sr. nurse, phsysician – ~£100k/year)
- **Evidence**: Observational – 40–50% conversion from A&E attendance to admission avoided (NHS England 2022)
- **Timeline**: 6 months to commission space and staff
- **Barries**: need diportation for patients; out‑of‑hours coverage
- **Metrics**: number of respiratory admissions averted; 7‑day return rate

**Intervention 4.2: Same‑day.obsessional & haematology SDEC for frail elderly**
- **Implementation level**: as above
- **Evidence**: weaker but increasing

